 Top of the Morning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Private markets have a history of following public markets, with a lag.

 We might be in the midst of that lag. Driving the news: U.S. tech stocks lost trillions of dollars yesterday, with the Nasdaq Composite suffering its worst percentage decline since President Trump unveiled his "Liberation Day" tariffs.

 A lot of fingers were understandably pointed at the midterm results, given how many Democrats want to slow data center construction and take other measures that could curb AI growth. But, to be honest, no one really knows what happened yesterday. Stocks rallied on Wednesday — when the blue tsunami was known — and the Nasdaq is rebounding this morning. The big picture: What's more important than this week is this year.

 The Nasdaq is up around 6% year-to-date and down more than 4% over the past six months. Does that even sort of track what you're seeing in skyrocketing private tech company valuations? Obviously it's not apples-to-apples — particularly when looking at high-growth startups — but the directions should track. At least eventually (i.e., the lag). Zoom in: An irony is that many Americans just voted against AI acceleration at a time when their financial exposure is about to increase.

 Alternative investments like private equity and private credit are heading for 401(k)s and, to a much lesser impact, "Trump Accounts" may get stuffed with donated tech stocks. Look ahead: The question for private market investors is if they believe past is precedent, or if they bet that this time is different, because AI is a more transformational technology than almost any that has come before it.

 One big bellwether should be the Anthropic IPO, whose roadshow is likely to begin in a week or so. If it cuts back its lofty valuation expectations, investors might gain courage in deal negotiations. 
 
